          "Evaluate commercial offers on the same assumptions about condition, testing and evidence. Ask how initial values are determined, which deductions are possible, how failures are documented and when the business can challenge a regrade. Separate service charges from proposed resale proceeds so a headline recovery estimate does not obscure transport, media treatment or low-value residual processing. Clarify who owns equipment during assessment, when title is intended to transfer under the contract and who bears relevant custody risks. Those questions should be reviewed by procurement and legal staff, not inferred from an invoice. A supplier's willingness to buy equipment is not itself evidence that the proposed reuse route is suitable or approved.",
          "Write an asset-level change process into the agreement. Specify who may approve a move from resale to recycling, what happens to unsold equipment and whether the provider can substitute downstream partners. Include confidentiality, access to agreed records, incident notification, dispute handling and responsibilities when the relationship ends. Request a redacted example of the final report and compare its fields with your inventory and security requirements. Establish milestones for assessment, approval, settlement and evidence that depend on actual readiness and capacity rather than guaranteed universal turnaround. Keep supplier selection reasons on record, including how control weaknesses were resolved, so a future procurement review can distinguish an informed decision from a choice based only on promised recovery value.",
