          "Ask automation and security personnel to assess controllers, operator terminals, test equipment and recording devices for stored recipes, logs, intellectual property or personal information. Retirement approval should confirm that required production records and recovery information remain available in an approved retained location, with legal holds considered before irreversible treatment. Qualified personnel should select media-appropriate sanitization and acceptance criteria, taking account of equipment condition and any restrictions on powering or handling it. Do not ask recycling contractors to browse proprietary process files or receive operational credentials simply to value equipment. Safety assessment and information protection must be coordinated so an unsafe device is not energised merely to attempt data preparation before collection.",
          "Reconcile the released inventory with loading records, receiver acceptance, media results where required and final agreed treatment evidence. Keep environmental records distinct from technical sanitization acceptance and from the commercial settlement for recoverable materials. Record partial rejection, revised classification, unidentifiable modules and missing data evidence as separate issues with responsible owners. Preserve the explanation and approval for corrections instead of replacing the original register without history. Agree record retention and access with the appropriate legal, safety, security and records teams according to applicable requirements and contracts. Internal review should trace selected items from the original process area to final outcome and identify anything reported complete without adequate release authority or receiving evidence.",
